  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe deep upper low, which brought last night's heavy snow to eastern \nMissouri and today's very strong winds over the East Coast, is \npulling away from eastern Kansas this afternoon. Expecting one more \ncold night with lows in the 20s, especially as skies clear and north \nwinds become near calm. By tomorrow, winds will turn southwesterly \nas high pressure moves off to the east. Coupled with plenty of \nsunshine, this will allow temperatures to warm around or above 50. \n\nAfter another seasonable day Saturday, our next chance for rain will \narrive Saturday night. Model guidance remains consistent in another \namplifying shortwave trough diving southeast across the central \nPlains, driving a surface low and associated cold front east across \nthe area. Not expecting heavy precipitation given an initially \npositively tilted trough and a weaker 850 mb low, but strengthening \nmid-level frontogenesis should support a swath of 0.2-0.4\" of rain. \nAs CAA amplifies on the back side of the low, still can't rule out a \nbit of wet snow as precipitation ends. Model soundings continue to\nhave an isothermal layer around 925 mb, which could be enough for a\nfew flakes to mix in, or even a brief changeover. Regardless, any \naccumulation remains quite unlikely, at most perhaps a bit on grassy \nsurfaces north of I-70.\n\nTemperatures will be cooler for Sunday behind the front, with highs \nback in the 40s area wide. We gradually trend warmer again \nafterwards, as broad southerly flow develops ahead of a deep upper \nlow moves towards the Southwest CONUS. Highs may warm into the 60s \nby Wednesday, followed by returning rain chances as the upper low \napproaches around Thursday.",
